summary:
This is the second page of the book's table of contents (printed page vii in the page header). It lists all of **chapter 2, "The mathematical building blocks of neural networks" (printed p.16, PDF p.40)** and the first five sections of **chapter 3, "Introduction to TensorFlow, PyTorch, JAX, and Keras" (printed p.60, PDF p.84)**.

**Chapter 2** moves from a concrete example to theory and back:
- 2.1 a first look at a neural network (the MNIST digit classifier);
- 2.2 data representations — tensors of rank 0, 1, 2, 3 and higher, their key attributes, NumPy manipulation, batches, and real-world data tensors;
- 2.3 tensor operations — element-wise ops, broadcasting, the tensor (dot) product, reshaping, and a geometric interpretation of tensor operations and of deep learning;
- 2.4 gradient-based optimization — derivatives, gradients, stochastic gradient descent, and backpropagation;
- 2.5 a look back at the first example, reimplemented from scratch (one training step, the full loop, evaluation).

**Chapter 3** begins with a brief history of deep learning frameworks (3.1) and how they relate to each other (3.2), then introduces **TensorFlow (3.3), PyTorch (3.4) and JAX (3.5)**. Each framework section follows the same template: first steps, an end-to-end example (a linear classifier written in that framework alone), and "what makes the … approach unique". The JAX section additionally covers tensors in JAX and random number generation, which works differently in JAX. Section 3.6 (Keras) continues on the next contents page.

Use this page as a map: if a later chapter mentions gradients or broadcasting and you need a refresher, the subsection entries here point you straight to it.
